1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,837
Chapter 24: Intrapartum Assessment.

3
00:00:05,837 --> 00:00:25,600
Electronic Fetal Monitoring Other Intrapartum Assessment Techniques Nonreassuring Fetal Status Meconium In The Amnionic Fluid Current Recommendations Intrapartum Surveillance Of Uterine Activity References Electronic fetal monitoring eclipsed periodic fetoscopic auscultation of the fetal heartbeat in the late 1960s .

4
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:36,750
It was hoped that the continuous graph-paper portrayal of the fetal heart rate (FHR), termed cardiotocography, would reflect pathophysiological events affecting the fetus.

5
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:43,287
Initially, electronic FHR monitoring was used primarily in complicated pregnancies.

6
00:00:43,287 --> 00:00:50,712
Now, more than 85 percent of all live births in the United States undergo electronic FHR monitoring .

7
00:00:50,712 --> 00:00:53,362
Electronic Fetal Monitoring.

8
00:00:53,362 --> 00:00:57,637
# Internal (Direct) Electronic Monitoring.

9
00:00:57,637 --> 00:01:04,612
Direct FHR measurement is accomplished by attaching a bipolar spiral electrode directly to the fetus .

10
00:01:04,612 --> 00:01:11,062
The wire electrode penetrates the fetal scalp, and the second pole is a metal wing on the electrode.

11
00:01:11,062 --> 00:01:21,487
The P wave, QRS complex, and T wave of the electrical fetal cardiac signal are amplified and fed into a cardiotachometer for heart rate calculation.

12
00:01:21,487 --> 00:01:28,387
The peak R-wave voltage is the most reliably detected portion of the fetal electrocardiogram (ECG).

13
00:01:28,387 --> 00:01:38,200
Time (t) in milliseconds between fetal R waves is fed into a cardiotachometer, and a new FHR is set with the arrival of each new R wave .

14
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:47,437
The phenomenon of continuous R-to-R wave FHR computation was known as beat-to-beat variability, and is now called baseline variability.

15
00:01:47,437 --> 00:01:59,600
If present, fetal premature atrial contractions (PACs) cause the cardiotachometer to rapidly and erratically seek new heart rates and create the “spiking” shown Figure 24-3.

16
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:02,150
Figure 24-1.

17
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:11,337
With internal electronic fetal monitoring a bipolar electrode is attached to the fetal scalp for detection of fetal QRS complexes (F).

18
00:02:11,337 --> 00:02:17,362
At times, the maternal heart and its electrical complex (M) also may be detected.

19
00:02:17,362 --> 00:02:19,862
Figure 24-2.

20
00:02:19,862 --> 00:02:26,887
Fetal electrocardiographic signals from the scalp electrode are used to compute continuous beat-to-beat heart rate.

21
00:02:26,887 --> 00:02:38,337
Time intervals (t1, t2, t3) in milliseconds between successive fetal R waves are used by a cardiotachometer to compute instantaneous fetal heart rate.

22
00:02:38,337 --> 00:02:45,937
In this example, the t2 interval has complexes that are close together, which indicates a slighted higher heart rate.

23
00:02:45,937 --> 00:02:50,462
This is reflected in the vertical rise on the cardiotachometer graph.

24
00:02:50,462 --> 00:02:56,475
ECG = electrocardiogram; PAC = premature atrial contraction.

25
00:02:56,475 --> 00:02:59,037
Figure 24-3.

26
00:02:59,037 --> 00:03:04,075
Standard fetal monitor tracing of heart rate using a fetal scalp electrode.

27
00:03:04,075 --> 00:03:08,875
Spiking of the fetal rate reflects premature atrial contractions.

28
00:03:08,875 --> 00:03:15,287
Electrical cardiac complexes detected by the fetal electrode include those generated by the mother.

29
00:03:15,287 --> 00:03:22,400
However, the amplitude of the maternal ECG signal is diminished when recorded through the fetal scalp electrode.

30
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:27,500
Thus, maternal cardiac signals do not appear on the FHR tracing.

31
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:37,200
However, if the fetus is dead, maternal R waves can be still detected by the scalp electrode as the next best signal and are counted by the cardiotachometer .

32
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:39,887
Figure 24-4.

33
00:03:39,887 --> 00:03:41,975
Placental abruption.

34
00:03:41,975 --> 00:03:46,562
The fetal scalp electrode first detects the dying fetus’ heart rate.

35
00:03:46,562 --> 00:03:52,550
After fetal death, the maternal electrocardiogram complex is detected and recorded.

36
00:03:52,550 --> 00:03:56,962
# External (Indirect) Electronic Monitoring.

37
00:03:56,962 --> 00:04:04,525
Although it avoids membrane rupture, external monitoring does not provide the precision of internal FHR monitoring .

38
00:04:04,525 --> 00:04:09,162
Moreover, in some women, external monitoring may be difficult.

39
00:04:09,162 --> 00:04:16,650
For example, fetuses of obese women have a greater amount of unmonitored time compared with those of normal-weight women .

40
00:04:16,650 --> 00:04:24,437
With external monitoring, the FHR is detected through the maternal abdominal wall using the ultrasound Doppler principle .

41
00:04:24,437 --> 00:04:32,375
The instrument contains a transducer that emits ultrasound and a sensor to detect a shift in frequency of the reflected sound.

42
00:04:32,375 --> 00:04:38,687
The transducer is placed on the maternal abdomen at a site where fetal heart action is best detected.

43
00:04:38,687 --> 00:04:43,925
A coupling gel must be applied because air conducts ultrasound waves poorly.

44
00:04:43,925 --> 00:04:47,575
The device is held in position by an elastic belt.

45
00:04:47,575 --> 00:04:49,412
Ultrasound

46
00:04:49,564 --> 00:04:54,539
Doppler signals are edited electronically before FHR data appear.

47
00:04:54,489 --> 00:05:04,176
Reflected ultrasound signals from moving fetal heart valves are analyzed through a microprocessor that compares incoming signals with the most recent previous signal.

48
00:05:04,176 --> 00:05:13,614
This process, called autocorrelation, is based on the premise that the FHR has regularity, whereas “noise” is random and lacks regularity.

49
00:05:13,614 --> 00:05:21,276
Several fetal heart motions must be deemed electronically acceptable by the microprocessor before the FHR is revealed.

50
00:05:21,276 --> 00:05:25,689
Such electronic editing has greatly improved tracing quality.

51
00:05:25,689 --> 00:05:34,201
Many fetal monitors are capable of interfacing with archival storage systems, which obviates maintaining actual paper tracings.

52
00:05:34,201 --> 00:05:47,076
Other features of current external monitors include the ability to monitor twin fetuses, concurrently assess maternal heart rate, display fetal ECG, and record maternal pulse oximetry values.

53
00:05:47,076 --> 00:05:59,739
Wireless, abdominal, dermal-patch, external array systems may improve the interpretability of intrapartum FHR tracings in obese women (Lempersz, 2020; Monson, 2020).

54
00:05:59,739 --> 00:06:08,126
Wireless monitors use electrocardiography to accurately measure maternal and fetal heart rates yet allow patient mobility during labor.

55
00:06:08,126 --> 00:06:11,064
# Fetal Heart Rate Patterns.

56
00:06:11,064 --> 00:06:17,414
The interpretation of FHR patterns can be problematic without definitions and nomenclature.

57
00:06:17,414 --> 00:06:24,626
In one study, three Maternal-Fetal Medicine specialists independently interpreted FHR tracings.

58
00:06:24,626 --> 00:06:30,464
Agreement was poor for the most ominous tracings and was moderate for less severe patterns .

59
00:06:30,464 --> 00:06:38,489
To help standardize definitions, the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (NICHD) convened a working group .

60
00:06:38,489 --> 00:06:47,689
The resulting definitions are used in this chapter and have been adopted by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a).

61
00:06:47,689 --> 00:06:52,951
Interobserver agreement improves when these standardized definitions are implemented .

62
00:06:52,951 --> 00:06:55,501
Table 24-1.

63
00:06:55,501 --> 00:07:04,101
Electronic Fetal Monitoring Definitions Pattern interpretation derives from the FHR that is portrayed on the monitor or graph paper.

64
00:07:04,101 --> 00:07:09,376
Thus, the choice of vertical and horizontal scaling greatly affects patterns.

65
00:07:09,376 --> 00:07:17,776
NICHD Workshop scaling parameters recommend that each centimeter on the vertical axis represents 30 beats per minute (bpm).

66
00:07:17,776 --> 00:07:23,064
Measurement markers on this axis range from 30 to 240 bpm.

67
00:07:23,064 --> 00:07:29,251
Along the horizontal axis, the chart recorder moves at a speed of 3 cm/min.

68
00:07:29,251 --> 00:07:35,901
The time between each bold vertical line on this axis is 1 minute (see Fig. 24-4).

69
00:07:35,901 --> 00:07:41,126
FHR patterns during labor are dynamic and can interchange rapidly.

70
00:07:41,126 --> 00:08:02,614
Although intrapartum evaluation of fetal heart tracings occur every 5 to 30 minutes, tracing interpretation should reflect a longitudinal assessment beyond the current 5- to 30-minute segment (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019b; Robinson, 2008; Vintzileos, 2016).

71
00:08:02,614 --> 00:08:08,501
Thus, a full description should include an evaluation of changes or trends over time .

72
00:08:08,501 --> 00:08:11,776
# Baseline Fetal Heart Activity.

73
00:08:11,776 --> 00:08:18,989
This refers to FHR baseline characteristics apart from periodic accelerations or decelerations.

74
00:08:18,989 --> 00:08:26,339
Elements include rate, variability, and distinct FHR patterns such as sinusoidal or saltatory.

75
00:08:26,339 --> 00:08:36,351
Rate Beginning at 16 weeks’ gestation, the heart rate drops approximately 1 bpm each week (Pillai, 1990; Serra 2009).

76
00:08:36,351 --> 00:08:42,626
This continues postnatally such that the average rate is 85 bpm by age 8 years .

77
00:08:42,626 --> 00:08:50,801
This normal gradual slowing of the FHR is thought to correspond to maturation of parasympathetic (vagal) heart control .

78
00:08:50,801 --> 00:08:58,826
The baseline FHR is the approximate mean rate during a 10-minute tracing segment and is rounded to increments of 5 bpm.

79
00:08:58,826 --> 00:09:08,126
In any 10-minute window, the minimum interpretable baseline duration must be at least 2 minutes—otherwise the baseline is considered indeterminate.

80
00:09:08,126 --> 00:09:14,914
For an indeterminate baseline, the previous 10-minute interval is used to determine the baseline heart rate.

81
00:09:14,914 --> 00:09:20,989
If the baseline FHR is <110 bpm, it is termed bradycardia.

82
00:09:20,989 --> 00:09:26,876
If the baseline rate is >160 bpm, it is called tachycardia.

83
00:09:26,876 --> 00:09:35,889
The average FHR is considered the result of tonic balance between accelerator and decelerator influences on cardiac pacemaker cells.

84
00:09:35,889 --> 00:09:40,164
In this concept, the sympathetic system is the accelerator

85
00:09:40,324 --> 00:09:42,049
influence.

86
00:09:41,999 --> 00:09:48,299
The parasympathetic system is the decelerator factor and mediated by vagal slowing of heart rate .

87
00:09:48,299 --> 00:09:56,349
Heart rate also is under the control of arterial chemoreceptors such that both hypoxia and hypercapnia can modulate rate.

88
00:09:56,349 --> 00:10:05,299
Prolonged hypoxia that is associated with a rising blood lactate level and severe metabolic acidemia induces an extended drop in heart rate .

89
00:10:05,299 --> 00:10:07,174
Bradycardia.

90
00:10:07,174 --> 00:10:15,149
In the third trimester, the normal mean baseline FHR ranges between 110 and 160 bpm.

91
00:10:15,149 --> 00:10:26,349
But, pragmatically, a rate between 100 and 119 bpm, in the absence of other FHR changes, usually is not representative of fetal compromise.

92
00:10:26,349 --> 00:10:34,536
Such mild bradycardias were observed in 2 percent of monitored pregnancies and averaged approximately 50 minutes in duration .

93
00:10:34,536 --> 00:10:44,661
These low baseline heart rates have also been attributed to head compression from occiput posterior or transverse positions, particularly during second-stage labor.

94
00:10:44,661 --> 00:10:57,149
However, fetal bradycardia may stem from congenital heart block or from fetal hypoxia due to maternal or fetal causes (Bravo-Valenzuela, 2018; Lepercq, 2021).

95
00:10:57,149 --> 00:11:04,086
For example, Figure 24-4 shows bradycardia in a fetus dying from placental abruption.

96
00:11:04,086 --> 00:11:15,374
Less often, sustained maternal hypothermia also can cause fetal bradycardia, but these uniquely involved fetuses apparently are not harmed by several hours of such bradycardia .

97
00:11:15,374 --> 00:11:17,274
Tachycardia.

98
00:11:17,274 --> 00:11:22,749
This is defined as a baseline FHR >160 bpm.

99
00:11:22,749 --> 00:11:26,286
The most common explanation is maternal fever.

100
00:11:26,286 --> 00:11:31,561
In some cases, fetal tachycardia may precede overt maternal fever .

101
00:11:31,561 --> 00:11:43,136
Fetal tachycardia caused by maternal infection typically is not associated with fetal compromise unless it is associated with fetal sepsis or with significant FHR decelerations.

102
00:11:43,136 --> 00:11:56,161
Other causes of fetal tachycardia include fetal compromise, cardiac arrhythmias, and maternal administration of parasympathetic inhibiting (atropine) or sympathomimetic (terbutaline) drugs.

103
00:11:56,161 --> 00:12:05,111
Prompt treatment of the compromising event, such as alleviation of maternal fever or volume resuscitation, can result in fetal recovery.

104
00:12:05,111 --> 00:12:13,299
The key feature to distinguish fetal compromise in association with tachycardia seems to be concomitant FHR decelerations .

105
00:12:13,299 --> 00:12:24,211
Baseline Variability This is an important index of cardiovascular function and reflects a sympathetic and parasympathetic “push and pull” mediated by the fetal sinoatrial node.

106
00:12:24,211 --> 00:12:28,849
This produces beat-to-beat fluctuations of the baseline FHR .

107
00:12:28,849 --> 00:12:35,074
Variability describes these changes during 1 minute, which modify the baseline’s waviness .

108
00:12:35,074 --> 00:12:39,911
Interpretation of variability is subjective and judges this waviness.

109
00:12:39,911 --> 00:12:48,936
The NICHD workshop defined baseline variability as irregular fluctuations in the baseline excluding accelerations and decelerations .

110
00:12:48,936 --> 00:12:53,736
They recommended the criteria shown in Figure 24-5.

111
00:12:53,736 --> 00:12:56,686
for quantification of variability.

112
00:12:56,686 --> 00:13:02,124
Normal variability shows oscillations that change 6 to 25 bpm.

113
00:13:02,124 --> 00:13:10,924
FHR variability increases with advancing gestational age (Serra, 2009; Shuffrey, 2019).

114
00:13:10,924 --> 00:13:13,686
Figure 24-5.

115
00:13:13,686 --> 00:13:18,699
Baseline fetal heart rate variability shown in the following five panels.

116
00:13:18,699 --> 00:13:22,361
1. Undetectable, absent variability.

117
00:13:22,361 --> 00:13:27,074
2. Minimal variability, ≤5 bpm.

118
00:13:27,074 --> 00:13:32,624
3. Moderate (normal) variability, 6 to 25 bpm.

119
00:13:32,624 --> 00:13:37,236
4. Marked variability, >25 bpm.

120
00:13:37,236 --> 00:13:40,074
5. Sinusoidal pattern.

121
00:13:40,074 --> 00:13:49,761
This differs from variability in that it has a smooth, sinelike pattern of regular fluctuation and is excluded in the definition of fetal heart rate variability.

122
00:13:49,761 --> 00:13:52,099
Increased Variability.

123
00:13:52,099 --> 00:13:57,286
Several physiological and pathological processes affect variability.

124
00:13:57,286 --> 00:14:05,236
Greater variability accompanies fetal breathing and body movements (Dawes, 1981; Zizzo, 2020).

125
00:14:05,236 --> 00:14:17,511
Shuffrey and colleagues (2020) showed the FHR had enhanced variation in state 4F (active awake state) compared with 1F (quiet state) and 2F (active state) .

126
00:14:17,511 --> 00:14:30,736
However, in one study of 390 fetuses, greater intrapartum variability was associated with abnormal fetal arterial cord blood gas measurements but without increased neonatal morbidity rates .

127
00:14:30,736 --> 00:14:33,061
Decreased Variability.

128
00:14:33,061 --> 00:14:41,561
Absent variability and minimal variability are defined as no baseline fluctuation or changes ≤5 bpm.

129
00:14:41,561 --> 00:14:45,099
These are shown in Figure 24-5.

130
00:14:45,099 --> 00:14:47,511
panels 1 and 2.

131
00:14:47,511 --> 00:14:51,461
Of greatest concern, diminished variability can

132
00:14:51,628 --> 00:14:56,290
be an ominous sign that indicates a seriously compromised fetus.

133
00:14:56,240 --> 00:15:04,653
According to Esplin (2020), metabolic acidemia depresses the fetal brainstem or heart to create the loss of variability.

134
00:15:04,653 --> 00:15:12,615
Thus, diminished variability, when it reflects fetal compromise, likely reflects acidemia rather than hypoxia.

135
00:15:12,615 --> 00:15:21,065
Severe maternal acidemia also can lower fetal variability, for example, in a mother with diabetic ketoacidosis.

136
00:15:21,065 --> 00:15:27,528
Reduced baseline FHR variability is the single most reliable sign of fetal compromise.

137
00:15:27,528 --> 00:15:34,453
Moderate variability is associated with a normal umbilical cord pH in 98 percent of cases .

138
00:15:34,453 --> 00:15:50,090
Absent or minimal variability is associated with fetal acidemia, but it predicts an umbilical cord pH <7.15 in only 12 to 31 percent of cases (Parer, 2006; Williams, 2003).

139
00:15:50,090 --> 00:16:00,415
In a study of 2200 consecutive deliveries, authors concluded that variability by itself could not be used as the only indicator of fetal well-being .

140
00:16:00,415 --> 00:16:11,340
Comorbid fetal tachycardia or FHR decelerations also may help predict fetal acidemia (Esplin, 2020; Vintzileos, 2016).

141
00:16:11,340 --> 00:16:17,953
Another common cause of diminished variability is administration of some analgesic drugs during labor .

142
00:16:17,953 --> 00:16:27,403
These can depress the central nervous system and include narcotics, barbiturates, phenothiazines, tranquilizers, and general anesthetics.

143
00:16:27,403 --> 00:16:44,103
As one specific example, variability regularly diminishes within 5 to 10 minutes following intravenous (IV) meperidine administration, and the effects may last 60 minutes or longer (Hill, 2003; Petrie, 1993).

144
00:16:44,103 --> 00:16:47,190
IV butorphanol has similar effects .

145
00:16:47,190 --> 00:16:52,615
And, chronically administered buprenorphine suppresses FHR and movement .

146
00:16:52,615 --> 00:16:57,990
Corticosteroids also decrease fetal movement and thereby dampen variability .

147
00:16:57,990 --> 00:17:09,065
Magnesium sulfate can diminish variability and is widely used in the United States for fetal neuroprotection, tocolysis, and seizure prophylaxis in preeclampsia.

148
00:17:09,065 --> 00:17:18,765
In a study of nearly 250 term gestations, its administration led to decreased variability but without evidence of adverse neonatal effects .

149
00:17:18,765 --> 00:17:25,328
Others echo these findings (Nensi, 2014; Verdurmen, 2017).

150
00:17:25,328 --> 00:17:32,603
In sum, variability is affected by fetal physiology, and its meaning differs depending on clinical setting.

151
00:17:32,603 --> 00:17:38,978
A decrease in variability but without decelerations is unlikely to reflect fetal hypoxia .

152
00:17:38,978 --> 00:17:51,565
A persistently flat FHR baseline—absent variability—within the normal baseline rate range and without decelerations may reflect a prior fetal insult that has resulted in neurological damage .

153
00:17:51,565 --> 00:18:07,990
Cardiac Arrhythmia When fetal cardiac arrhythmias are first suspected using electronic monitoring, findings can include baseline bradycardia, tachycardia, or most commonly in our experience, abrupt baseline spiking (see Fig. 24-3).

154
00:18:07,990 --> 00:18:14,203
An arrhythmia can only be documented, practically speaking, when scalp electrodes are used.

155
00:18:14,203 --> 00:18:20,790
Some fetal monitors can be adapted to output the scalp electrode signals into an ECG recorder.

156
00:18:20,790 --> 00:18:28,640
Because only a single lead is obtained, analysis and interpretation of rhythm and rate disturbances are severely limited.

157
00:18:28,640 --> 00:18:38,053
In one study, rate and rhythm disturbances in 934 normal pregnancies were evaluated between 30 and 40 weeks’ gestation.

158
00:18:38,053 --> 00:18:47,615
Arrhythmias, episodes of bradycardia <100 bpm, or tachycardia >180 bpm were encountered in 3 percent .

159
00:18:47,615 --> 00:18:57,090
Most supraventricular arrhythmias carry little significance during labor unless fetal heart failure, as evidenced by hydrops, is coexistent.

160
00:18:57,090 --> 00:19:05,903
Many supraventricular arrhythmias disappear in the immediate neonatal period, although some are associated with structural cardiac defects .

161
00:19:05,903 --> 00:19:11,128
Intermittent baseline bradycardia is frequently due to congenital heart block.

162
00:19:11,128 --> 00:19:21,140
Conduction defects, most often complete atrioventricular (AV) block, usually are found in association with maternal connective tissue diseases .

163
00:19:21,140 --> 00:19:30,365
Antepartum evaluation of the fetus with an identified arrhythmia and treatment options are discussed in Chapter 19 (p. 368).

164
00:19:30,365 --> 00:19:42,040
Most fetal arrhythmias without comorbid fetal hydrops are inconsequential during labor, but they may hinder interpretation of FHR tracings and thereby necessitate cesarean delivery.

165
00:19:42,040 --> 00:19:47,740
Sonographic evaluation of fetal anatomy and echocardiography can be useful.

166
00:19:47,908 --> 00:19:55,895
Generally, without evidence of fetal hydrops, neonatal outcome is not measurably improved by pregnancy intervention.

167
00:19:55,845 --> 00:20:05,883
At Parkland Hospital, intrapartum fetal cardiac arrhythmias, especially those associated with clear amnionic fluid, are typically managed conservatively.

168
00:20:05,883 --> 00:20:16,408
Sinusoidal Heart Rate A sinusoidal baseline is one with a sine wave–like undulating pattern with a frequency of 3 to 5 cycles per minute for at least 20 minutes.

169
00:20:16,408 --> 00:20:28,570
A true sinusoidal pattern such as that shown in panel 5 of Figure 24-5 can be observed with fetal intracranial hemorrhage, severe fetal asphyxia, or severe fetal anemia.

170
00:20:28,570 --> 00:20:39,108
The last may stem from alloimmunization, fetomaternal hemorrhage, twin-twin transfusion syndrome, fetal parvoviral infection, or vasa previa with bleeding.

171
00:20:39,108 --> 00:20:45,783
The pathophysiology of sinusoidal patterns is unclear, in part due to varying definitions.

172
00:20:45,783 --> 00:20:54,745
One group proposed that the pattern relates to fetal arterial blood pressure and oscillations in the baroreceptor-chemoreceptor feedback mechanism .

173
00:20:54,745 --> 00:21:04,708
Modanlou (1982, 2004) proposed adoption of a strict definition for true sinusoidal FHR that is most likely ominous.

174
00:21:04,708 --> 00:21:09,370
This criteria eliminates the influence of maternal medications.

175
00:21:09,370 --> 00:21:17,245
1. Stable baseline FHR of 120 to 160 bpm with regular oscillations 2.

176
00:21:17,245 --> 00:21:21,945
Amplitude of 5 to 15 bpm (rarely greater) 3.

177
00:21:21,945 --> 00:21:24,808
2 to 5 cycles per minute 4.

178
00:21:24,808 --> 00:21:28,545
Absent variability and accelerations 5.

179
00:21:28,545 --> 00:21:33,695
Oscillation of the sinusoidal waveform above or below a baseline 6.

180
00:21:33,695 --> 00:21:49,883
No concurrent drugs such as narcotics Other investigators classify sinusoidal heart rate patterns into mild— amplitude 5 to 15 bpm; intermediate—16 to 24 bpm; and major—≥25 bpm.

181
00:21:49,883 --> 00:21:57,995
Increasing amplitude correlates with advancing fetal risk (Murphy, 1991; Neesham, 1993).

182
00:21:57,995 --> 00:22:12,820
Importantly, insignificant sinusoidal patterns have been reported following administration of meperidine, morphine, alphaprodine, and butorphanol (Angel, 1984; Egley, 1991; Epstein, 1982).

183
00:22:12,820 --> 00:22:18,570
When due to narcotics, this pattern has a sine frequency of 6 cycles per minute.

184
00:22:18,570 --> 00:22:26,608
A sinusoidal pattern also has been described with chorioamnionitis, fetal distress, and umbilical cord occlusion .

185
00:22:26,608 --> 00:22:38,570
Investigators have concluded that intrapartum sinusoidal fetal heart patterns are not generally associated with fetal compromise (Johnson, 1981; Young, 1980a).

186
00:22:38,570 --> 00:22:43,045
Thus, management is usually dictated by the clinical setting.

187
00:22:43,045 --> 00:22:50,533
Pseudosinusoidal describes intrapartum sine wave–like baseline variation coupled with periods of acceleration.

188
00:22:50,533 --> 00:22:55,808
In one study, this pattern was seen in 15 percent of monitored labors .

189
00:22:55,808 --> 00:23:02,395
Mild pseudosinusoidal patterns were associated with use of meperidine and epidural analgesia.

190
00:23:02,395 --> 00:23:11,658
Intermediate pseudosinusoidal patterns were linked to fetal sucking or transient episodes of fetal hypoxia caused by umbilical cord compression.

191
00:23:11,658 --> 00:23:18,870
In another study, 4 percent of fetuses demonstrated sinusoidal patterns transiently during normal labor .

192
00:23:18,870 --> 00:23:23,858
These authors noted patterns lasting for 90 minutes in some cases.

193
00:23:23,858 --> 00:23:27,520
# Periodic Fetal Heart Rate Changes.

194
00:23:27,520 --> 00:23:31,745
These refer to visually apparent deviations from baseline.

195
00:23:31,745 --> 00:23:39,620
Acceleration refers to a rise in FHR above the baseline, and deceleration is a drop below the baseline rate.

196
00:23:39,620 --> 00:23:49,645
For the latter, terms used in the United States are early, late, or variable and refer to the temporal relationship between the deceleration and contractions.

197
00:23:49,645 --> 00:23:54,858
Waveform shapes also aid pattern recognition and are described subsequently.

198
00:23:54,858 --> 00:24:05,783
The NICHD Workshop (2008) considered decelerations to be recurrent if they accompanied ≥50 percent of contractions in any 20-minute period.

199
00:24:05,783 --> 00:24:14,220
Accelerations These are an abrupt FHR increase above the baseline and are defined by the onset-to-peak rise within 30 seconds .

200
00:24:14,220 --> 00:24:24,195
At ≥32 weeks’ gestation, an acceleration is one that peaks at a point ≥15 bpm above baseline.

201
00:24:24,195 --> 00:24:30,820
Its duration is ≥15 seconds but <2 minutes from onset to baseline return .

202
00:24:30,820 --> 00:24:39,508
Before 32 weeks, a peak ≥10 bpm and duration lasting 10 seconds to 2 minutes is considered normal.

203
00:24:39,508 --> 00:24:45,733
Prolonged acceleration is one lasting ≥2 minutes but <10 minutes.

204
00:24:45,733 --> 00:24:49,533
Accelerations occur antepartum and intrapartum.

205
00:24:49,533 --> 00:24:54,245
Proposed mechanisms for intrapartum accelerations include fetal

206
00:24:54,412 --> 00:25:04,574
movement, stimulation by uterine contractions, umbilical cord occlusion, fetal scalp stimulation, scalp blood sampling, and acoustic stimulation.

207
00:25:04,524 --> 00:25:07,587
Accelerations are common during labor.

208
00:25:07,587 --> 00:25:14,587
These are virtually always reassuring and almost always confirm that the fetus is not acidemic at that moment .

209
00:25:14,587 --> 00:25:22,299
Accelerations reflect intact neurohormonal cardiovascular control mechanisms linked to fetal behavioral states.

210
00:25:22,299 --> 00:25:31,787
In one analysis of FHR tracings in nearly 2000 fetuses, 99.8 percent showed sporadic accelerations during labor.

211
00:25:31,787 --> 00:25:39,049
FHR accelerations during the first or last 30 minutes of labor were a favorable sign for fetal well-being .

212
00:25:39,049 --> 00:25:47,887
Their absence during labor, however, is not necessarily an unfavorable sign unless coincidental with other nonreassuring changes.

213
00:25:47,887 --> 00:26:00,874
The chance of acidemia in the fetus that fails to respond to stimulation despite an otherwise nonreassuring pattern approximates 50 percent (Clark, 1984; Smith, 1986).

214
00:26:00,874 --> 00:26:09,537
Early Deceleration This physiological response shows a gradual FHR decline and then return to baseline that mirrors the contraction .

215
00:26:09,537 --> 00:26:18,512
Etiologically, head compression and dural stimulation probably cause vagal nerve activation, which mediates the FHR deceleration .

216
00:26:18,512 --> 00:26:28,974
Freeman and associates (2003) defined early decelerations as those generally seen in active labor between 4 and 7 cm cervical dilation.

217
00:26:28,974 --> 00:26:41,199
In their definition, the degree of deceleration is generally proportional to the contraction strength and rarely falls below 100 to 110 bpm or 20 to 30 bpm below baseline.

218
00:26:41,199 --> 00:26:50,424
These decelerations are common during active labor and not associated with tachycardia, loss of variability, or other FHR changes.

219
00:26:50,424 --> 00:26:58,737
Early decelerations are considered benign and not associated with fetal hypoxia, acidemia, or low Apgar scores.

220
00:26:58,737 --> 00:27:01,512
Figure 24-6.

221
00:27:01,512 --> 00:27:11,699
An early fetal heart rate deceleration characteristically shows a gradual decline and recovery in the heart rate that coincide with the onset and recovery of the contraction.

222
00:27:11,699 --> 00:27:18,249
The nadir of the deceleration is ≥30 seconds after the deceleration onset.

223
00:27:18,249 --> 00:27:30,724
Late Deceleration This deceleration is a smooth, gradual, symmetrical decline in the FHR that begins at or after the contraction peak and returns to baseline only after the contraction has ended.

224
00:27:30,724 --> 00:27:34,712
It reaches its nadir within 30 seconds of its onset .

225
00:27:34,712 --> 00:27:40,712
Typically, a late deceleration’s depth is <10 to 20 bpm below baseline.

226
00:27:40,712 --> 00:27:44,487
These usually are not accompanied by accelerations.

227
00:27:44,487 --> 00:27:47,224
Figure 24-7.

228
00:27:47,224 --> 00:27:57,924
A late fetal heart rate deceleration characteristically shows a gradual decline in the heart rate that starts at or after the contraction’s peak and recovers after the end of the contraction.

229
00:27:57,924 --> 00:28:04,574
The deceleration nadir occurs ≥30 seconds after the deceleration’s onset.

230
00:28:04,574 --> 00:28:10,337
Late decelerations often reflect poor uterine perfusion or placental dysfunction.

231
00:28:10,337 --> 00:28:22,937
In one animal study, uteroplacental perfusion was intentionally compromised, and the lag between contraction onset and late deceleration onset was directly related to basal fetal oxygenation .

232
00:28:22,937 --> 00:28:29,774
The lower the fetal PO2 before contractions, the shorter the lag to the late deceleration’s onset.

233
00:28:29,774 --> 00:28:40,974
This lag reflects the time necessary for the fetal PO2 to fall below a critical level necessary to stimulate arterial chemoreceptors, which mediate the decelerations.

234
00:28:40,974 --> 00:28:47,187
Late decelerations are the first FHR consequence of uteroplacentalinduced hypoxia.

235
00:28:47,187 --> 00:28:52,549
Fetal acidemia develops after frequent or prolonged periods of hypoxia.

236
00:28:52,549 --> 00:28:57,699
However, late decelerations alone do not predict fetal acidemia .

237
00:28:57,699 --> 00:29:15,662
Instead, late decelerations plus decreased variability, which itself is an indicator of less activity in the cardioregulatory center of the medulla, are more predictive of fetal acidemia and neonatal morbidity (Cahill, 2018; Holzmann, 2015).

238
00:29:15,662 --> 00:29:23,712
Cahill and coworkers (2018) calculated the total area within the below-baseline curves of decelerations.

239
00:29:23,712 --> 00:29:30,612
They found that increasing area was more predictive of fetal acidemia than other FHR characteristics.

240
00:29:30,612 --> 00:29:40,387
Generally, any process that produces maternal hypotension, excessive uterine activity, or placental dysfunction can induce late decelerations.

241
00:29:40,387 --> 00:29:45,187
The two most common are hypotension from epidural analgesia and

242
00:29:45,340 --> 00:29:49,377
uterine hyperactivity from oxytocin stimulation.

243
00:29:49,327 --> 00:29:56,727
Maternal hypertension, diabetes, and collagen vascular disorders can cause chronic placental dysfunction.

244
00:29:56,727 --> 00:30:01,027
Placental abruption can acutely produce late decelerations.

245
00:30:01,027 --> 00:30:09,465
Variable Deceleration This is the most frequent deceleration pattern encountered during labor and is attributed to umbilical cord occlusion.

246
00:30:09,465 --> 00:30:22,290
In a study of more than 7000 tracings, variable decelerations were identified in 40 percent when labor had progressed to 5 cm dilation and in 83 percent by the end of first-stage labor .

247
00:30:22,290 --> 00:30:28,240
The slope of FHR change is abrupt and erratic, giving the waveform its jagged shape .

248
00:30:28,240 --> 00:30:37,365
A variable deceleration is defined as a drop in the FHR that begins with the contraction’s onset and reaches a nadir in <30 seconds.

249
00:30:37,365 --> 00:30:46,077
The deceleration lasts between 15 seconds and 2 minutes, and its depth is ≥15 bpm in amplitude.

250
00:30:46,077 --> 00:30:51,052
The onset of deceleration typically varies with successive contractions.

251
00:30:51,052 --> 00:30:53,577
Figure 24-8.

252
00:30:53,577 --> 00:31:03,277
A variable fetal heart rate deceleration characteristically shows an abrupt drop in the heart rate, and its onset commonly varies with successive contractions.

253
00:31:03,277 --> 00:31:14,815
The deceleration depth measures ≥15 bpm, lasts ≥15 seconds, and has an onset-to-nadir phase <30 seconds.

254
00:31:14,815 --> 00:31:17,752
Its total duration is <2 minutes.

255
00:31:17,752 --> 00:31:24,265
Etiologically, variable deceleration often reflects some degree of umbilical cord occlusion.

256
00:31:24,265 --> 00:31:32,015
In one animal study, Hon (1959) assessed the effects of umbilical cord compression on FHR.

257
00:31:32,015 --> 00:31:37,440
He found lengthening and deepening of the deceleration curve with longer occlusion times.

258
00:31:37,440 --> 00:31:46,177
In another study, variable decelerations in fetal lambs occurred only after umbilical blood flow was reduced by at least 50 percent .

259
00:31:46,177 --> 00:31:51,515
Two patterns of variable decelerations are shown in Figure 24-9.

260
00:31:51,515 --> 00:31:59,415
The deceleration denoted by “A” is very much like that seen with complete umbilical cord occlusion in experimental animals.

261
00:31:59,415 --> 00:32:06,640
However, deceleration “B” differs and displays an acceleration before and after the deceleration component.

262
00:32:06,640 --> 00:32:14,202
Lee and coworkers (1975) proposed that this form was caused by differing degrees of cord occlusion.

263
00:32:14,202 --> 00:32:22,290
In this scheme, occlusion of only the vein reduces fetal blood return and thereby triggers a baroreceptor-mediated acceleration.

264
00:32:22,290 --> 00:32:32,040
With increasing intrauterine pressure and subsequent complete cord occlusion, fetal systemic hypertension develops due to obstructed umbilical artery flow.

265
00:32:32,040 --> 00:32:36,202
This stimulates a baroreceptor-mediated deceleration.

266
00:32:36,202 --> 00:32:42,190
Presumably, the aftercoming acceleration represents the same events occurring in reverse .

267
00:32:42,190 --> 00:32:53,952
Ball and Parer (1992) concluded that variable decelerations are mediated vagally and that the vagal response may be due to chemoreceptor or baroreceptor activity or both.

268
00:32:53,952 --> 00:33:03,990
Partial or complete cord occlusion produces an increase in afterload (baroreceptor) and a drop in fetal arterial oxygen content (chemoreceptor).

269
00:33:03,990 --> 00:33:08,015
Both result in vagal activity leading to deceleration.

270
00:33:08,015 --> 00:33:10,615
Figure 24-9.

271
00:33:10,615 --> 00:33:13,940
Variable fetal heart rate decelerations.

272
00:33:13,940 --> 00:33:20,227
Deceleration (B) exhibits “shoulders” of acceleration compared with deceleration (A).

273
00:33:20,227 --> 00:33:22,790
Figure 24-10.

274
00:33:22,790 --> 00:33:29,590
Schematic representation of the fetal heart rate effects with partial and complete umbilical cord occlusion.

275
00:33:29,590 --> 00:33:37,127
Uterine pressures generated early in a contraction cause cord compression predominantly of the thin-walled umbilical vein.

276
00:33:37,127 --> 00:33:44,015
The resulting decrease in fetal cardiac output leads to an initial compensatory rise in fetal heart rate.

277
00:33:44,015 --> 00:33:49,540
As cord compression intensifies, umbilical arteries are then also compressed.

278
00:33:49,540 --> 00:33:56,452
The resulting rise in fetal systolic blood pressure leads to a vagal-mediated fetal heart rate deceleration.

279
00:33:56,452 --> 00:34:06,290
As the contraction abates and compression is relieved first on the umbilical arteries, elevated fetal systolic blood pressures drop and the deceleration resolves.

280
00:34:06,290 --> 00:34:12,465
A final increase in fetal heart rate is seen as a result of persistent umbilical vein occlusion.

281
00:34:12,465 --> 00:34:19,040
With completion of the uterine contraction and cord compression, the fetal heart rate returns to baseline.

282
00:34:19,040 --> 00:34:21,615
BP = blood pressure.

283
00:34:21,615 --> 00:34:31,490
Thus, variable decelerations represent FHR reflexes that reflect either blood pressure changes due to interruption of umbilical flow or changes in

284
00:34:31,660 --> 00:34:33,547
oxygenation.

285
00:34:33,497 --> 00:34:42,072
It is likely that most fetuses have experienced brief but recurrent periods of hypoxia due to umbilical cord compression during gestation.

286
00:34:42,072 --> 00:34:51,185
The great dilemma for the obstetrician in managing variable FHR decelerations is determining when variable decelerations are pathological.

287
00:34:51,185 --> 00:35:05,560
According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a), recurrent variable decelerations with minimal to moderate variability are indeterminate, whereas those with absent variability are abnormal.

288
00:35:05,560 --> 00:35:12,297
A saltatory FHR baseline is another pattern linked to umbilical cord complications during labor .

289
00:35:12,297 --> 00:35:21,022
The pattern is rapidly recurring couplets of acceleration and deceleration causing relatively large oscillations of the FHR baseline.

290
00:35:21,022 --> 00:35:27,822
We also observed a relationship between cord occlusion and the saltatory pattern in postterm pregnancies .

291
00:35:27,822 --> 00:35:34,022
In the absence of other FHR findings, this pattern does not signal fetal compromise.

292
00:35:34,022 --> 00:35:36,647
Figure 24-11.

293
00:35:36,647 --> 00:35:43,935
Saltatory baseline fetal heart rate showing rapidly recurring couplets of acceleration combined with deceleration.

294
00:35:43,935 --> 00:35:56,685
Prolonged Deceleration This pattern is defined as an isolated deceleration with a depth >15 bpm and length ≥2 minutes but <10 minutes from onset to return to baseline .

295
00:35:56,685 --> 00:36:03,397
Prolonged decelerations are difficult to interpret because they are seen in many different clinical situations.

296
00:36:03,397 --> 00:36:11,497
Frequent causes are cervical examination, uterine hyperactivity, cord entanglement, and maternal supine hypotension.

297
00:36:11,497 --> 00:36:17,847
Epidural, spinal, or paracervical analgesia may induce a prolonged deceleration .

298
00:36:17,847 --> 00:36:26,897
Of women given epidural analgesia during labor in one analysis at Parkland Hospital, prolonged deceleration was noted in 1 percent .

299
00:36:26,897 --> 00:36:42,347
Other causes include placental abruption, umbilical cord knots or prolapse, maternal hypoperfusion or hypoxia from any cause, maternal seizures, fetal scalp-electrode application, impending birth, or maternal Valsalva maneuver.

300
00:36:42,347 --> 00:36:44,960
Figure 24-12.

301
00:36:44,960 --> 00:36:49,672
Prolonged fetal heart rate deceleration due to uterine hyperactivity.

302
00:36:49,672 --> 00:36:57,585
Approximately 3 minutes of the tracing are shown, but the fetal heart rate returned to normal after uterine hypertonus resolved.

303
00:36:57,585 --> 00:37:00,422
Vaginal delivery later ensued.

304
00:37:00,422 --> 00:37:06,710
The placenta can effectively resuscitate the fetus if the original insult does not recur immediately.

305
00:37:06,710 --> 00:37:17,172
Occasionally, such self-limited prolonged decelerations are followed by loss of variability, baseline tachycardia, and even a period of late decelerations.

306
00:37:17,172 --> 00:37:20,360
These all resolve as the fetus recovers.

307
00:37:20,360 --> 00:37:27,335
Freeman and colleagues (2003) emphasize that the fetus may die during prolonged decelerations.

308
00:37:27,335 --> 00:37:32,485
Thus, management of prolonged decelerations can be extremely tenuous.

309
00:37:32,485 --> 00:37:43,647
Management of isolated prolonged decelerations is based on bedside clinical judgment, which inevitably will sometimes be imperfect given the unpredictability of these decelerations.

310
00:37:43,647 --> 00:37:47,922
# Fetal Heart Rate Patterns During Second-stage Labor.

311
00:37:47,922 --> 00:37:52,572
Decelerations are virtually ubiquitous during the second-stage labor.

312
00:37:52,572 --> 00:38:00,222
In one study, only 1.4 percent of more than 7000 deliveries lacked decelerations during this stage .

313
00:38:00,222 --> 00:38:08,160
Both cord and fetal head compressions are implicated as causes of decelerations and of baseline bradycardia in this stage.

314
00:38:08,160 --> 00:38:18,710
In one series, profound, prolonged FHR deceleration in the 10 minutes preceding vaginal delivery was associated with intervention and good fetal outcomes .

315
00:38:18,710 --> 00:38:27,672
In a later extension of this series, similarly prolonged second-stage decelerations were also associated with a stillbirth and neonatal death .

316
00:38:27,672 --> 00:38:33,822
These experiences attest to the unpredictability of the FHR during second-stage labor.

317
00:38:33,822 --> 00:38:47,210
In the analysis noted earlier by Cahill and associates (2018), an increasing total deceleration area found in the final 120 minutes prior to delivery was most predictive of fetal acidemia.

318
00:38:47,210 --> 00:38:54,947
A combination of deceleration area plus a 10-minute period of tachycardia was most predictive of neonatal morbidity.

319
00:38:54,947 --> 00:39:01,585
Others have reported similar findings (Krebs, 1981; Picquard, 1988).

320
00:39:01,585 --> 00:39:15,135
Gull and colleagues (1996) observed that abrupt FHR decelerations to <100 bpm associated with lost variability for ≥4 minutes predicted fetal acidemia.

321
00:39:15,135 --> 00:39:17,585
Thus, second-stage

322
00:39:17,740 --> 00:39:28,940
decelerations plus an abnormal FHR baseline (bradycardia or tachycardia and/or absent variability) are associated with greater risk for fetal compromise .

323
00:39:28,890 --> 00:39:31,752
Figure 24-13.

324
00:39:31,752 --> 00:39:39,627
Cord-compression fetal heart rate decelerations in second-stage labor associated with tachycardia and loss of variability.

325
00:39:39,627 --> 00:39:43,890
The umbilical cord arterial pH was 6.9.

326
00:39:43,890 --> 00:39:47,065
# Computerized Interpretation.

327
00:39:47,065 --> 00:39:50,852
FHR pattern interpretations are subjective.

328
00:39:50,852 --> 00:39:58,477
Thus, the potential for computer assistance to enhance the precision of identifying abnormal patterns appeared promising.

329
00:39:58,477 --> 00:40:10,765
The INFANT Collaborative Group (2017) studied whether the addition of computer-based decision-support software for FHR pattern interpretation lowered the number of poor neonatal outcomes.

330
00:40:10,765 --> 00:40:19,065
In this large trial, women were randomly assigned to computer-assisted interpretation or to conventional clinical interpretation.

331
00:40:19,065 --> 00:40:32,602
Rates of adverse perinatal outcomes that included intrapartum stillbirth, early neonatal death, and neonatal encephalopathy were not improved, and a subset showed no difference in neurological development at 2 years.

332
00:40:32,602 --> 00:40:36,527
Cesarean delivery rates were similar in both groups.

333
00:40:36,527 --> 00:40:47,402
In a subsequent analysis of 71 adverse outcomes, computer interpretation of FHR tracings was deemed completely valid in only 34 percent of cases.

334
00:40:47,402 --> 00:40:54,652
Computer analysis missed a significant portion of decelerations, reduced variability, and tachysystole .

335
00:40:54,652 --> 00:40:57,677
Other Intrapartum Assessment Techniques.

336
00:40:57,677 --> 00:41:00,815
# Fetal Scalp Blood Sampling.

337
00:41:00,815 --> 00:41:12,852
According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a), measurements of the pH in capillary scalp blood may help identify the fetus in serious distress.

338
00:41:12,852 --> 00:41:21,390
However, this group also emphasizes that neither normal nor abnormal scalp pH results accurately predict neonatal outcome.

339
00:41:21,390 --> 00:41:27,940
Although used in other countries, the procedure is not available at most hospitals in the United States.

340
00:41:27,940 --> 00:41:36,902
With sampling, an illuminated endoscope is inserted through the dilated cervix after membrane rupture and is pressed firmly against the fetal scalp.

341
00:41:36,902 --> 00:41:45,352
The skin is wiped clean with a cotton swab and coated with a silicone gel, which allows fetal blood to accumulate as discrete globules.

342
00:41:45,352 --> 00:41:51,852
An incision is made through the fetal scalp to a depth of 2 mm with a special blade on a long handle.

343
00:41:51,852 --> 00:41:58,840
As a drop of blood forms on the surface, it is immediately collected into a heparinized glass capillary tube.

344
00:41:58,840 --> 00:42:01,740
The blood pH is measured promptly.

345
00:42:01,740 --> 00:42:10,427
The pH of fetal capillary scalp blood is usually lower than that of umbilical venous blood and approaches that of umbilical arterial blood.

346
00:42:10,427 --> 00:42:24,102
In one algorithm, if the pH is ≥7.25, labor is observed, and if between 7.20 and 7.25, the pH measurement is repeated within 30 minutes .

347
00:42:24,102 --> 00:42:34,965
If the pH is <7.20, another scalp blood sample is collected immediately, and delivery is performed promptly if the low pH level is confirmed.

348
00:42:34,965 --> 00:42:41,252
Otherwise, labor is allowed to continue, and scalp blood samples are repeated periodically.

349
00:42:41,252 --> 00:42:48,377
The only benefits reported for scalp blood pH testing are fewer cesarean deliveries for fetal distress .

350
00:42:48,377 --> 00:43:02,265
However, Goodwin and coworkers (1994) showed a decrease in the scalp pH sampling rate from approximately 1.8 percent in the mid-1980s to 0.03 percent by 1992.

351
00:43:02,265 --> 00:43:08,865
This drop in sampling rate was not associated with a higher cesarean delivery rate for fetal distress.

352
00:43:08,865 --> 00:43:13,415
They concluded that scalp blood pH sampling was unnecessary.

353
00:43:13,415 --> 00:43:21,540
Kruger and colleagues (1999) have assessed fetal scalp blood lactate concentration as an adjunct to pH.

354
00:43:21,540 --> 00:43:29,377
In one large randomized study, fetuses received scalp blood pH analysis or scalp blood lactate analysis .

355
00:43:29,377 --> 00:43:34,252
The authors found either to be equivalent in predicting fetal acidemia.

356
00:43:34,252 --> 00:43:44,277
In a secondary analysis, prediction of low Apgar scores or admission to the neonatal intensive care unit did not differ between lactate and pH groups .

357
00:43:44,277 --> 00:43:49,002
However, cesarean delivery rates were higher in the lactate group.

358
00:43:49,002 --> 00:43:51,715
# Scalp Stimulation.

359
00:43:51,715 --> 00:44:00,115
Clark and coworkers (1984) have suggested that fetal scalp stimulation is an alternative to scalp blood sampling.

360
00:44:00,115 --> 00:44:08,390
This proposal stemmed from the observation that FHR acceleration followed pinching the fetal scalp with an Allis clamp just before

361
00:44:08,548 --> 00:44:13,760
obtaining scalp blood and was invariably associated with a normal pH level.

362
00:44:13,710 --> 00:44:20,085
However, failure to provoke acceleration was not uniformly predictive of fetal acidemia.

363
00:44:20,085 --> 00:44:33,535
In another study of 58 cases in which the FHR accelerated >10 bpm after 15 seconds of digital scalp stroking, 100 percent had a scalp blood pH >7.20 .

364
00:44:33,535 --> 00:44:41,085
However, without an acceleration, only 30 percent had a scalp blood pH >7.20.

365
00:44:41,085 --> 00:44:49,873
Authors of a recent prospective study concluded that scalp stimulation was a reliable alternative to fetal scalp blood pH determination .

366
00:44:49,873 --> 00:44:57,873
In our practice, scalp stimulation is often one element of intrapartum assessment of the fetus with a category II tracing.

367
00:44:57,873 --> 00:45:01,085
# Vibroacoustic Stimulation.

368
00:45:01,085 --> 00:45:08,973
FHR acceleration in response to vibroacoustic stimulation is another substitute for fetal scalp blood sampling .

369
00:45:08,973 --> 00:45:16,360
The technique uses an electronic artificial larynx placed 1 cm from or directly onto the maternal abdomen .

370
00:45:16,360 --> 00:45:26,523
Response to vibroacoustic stimulation is considered normal if the FHR accelerates within 15 seconds after the stimulus and with prolonged fetal movements .

371
00:45:26,523 --> 00:45:38,860
Lin and colleagues (2001) prospectively studied vibroacoustic stimulation in 113 women in labor with either moderate to severe variable or late FHR decelerations.

372
00:45:38,860 --> 00:45:46,073
They concluded that this technique effectively predicts fetal acidosis in cases with variable decelerations.

373
00:45:46,073 --> 00:45:52,648
In the setting of late decelerations, its prediction of fetal acidosis, however, is limited.

374
00:45:52,648 --> 00:46:01,223
Other authors found vibroacoustic stimulation in second-stage labor did not predict neonatal outcome or enhance labor management .

375
00:46:01,223 --> 00:46:09,310
Skupski and coworkers (2002) performed a metaanalysis of reports on intrapartum fetal stimulation tests.

376
00:46:09,310 --> 00:46:19,335
These included fetal scalp puncture for blood pH testing, fetal scalp pinching, vibroacoustic stimulation, and digital stroking of the fetal scalp.

377
00:46:19,335 --> 00:46:22,573
Results were similar for all four methods.

378
00:46:22,573 --> 00:46:29,335
These authors concluded that intrapartum stimulation tests were useful to exclude fetal acidemia.

379
00:46:29,335 --> 00:46:33,773
They cautioned, however, that these tests are “less than perfect.

380
00:46:33,773 --> 00:46:36,685
# Fetal Pulse Oximetry.

381
00:46:36,685 --> 00:46:45,773
Using technology similar to that of adult pulse oximetry, this tool measures fetal oxyhemoglobin saturation once membranes are ruptured.

382
00:46:45,773 --> 00:46:51,648
A unique padlike sensor is inserted through the cervix and positioned against the fetal face.

383
00:46:51,648 --> 00:47:03,760
Using fetal pulse oximetry, the lower limit for normal fetal oxygen saturation is generally considered to be 30 percent (Gorenberg, 2003; Stiller, 2002).

384
00:47:03,760 --> 00:47:10,935
However, when measured in umbilical arterial blood, fetal oxygen saturation normally varies greatly.

385
00:47:10,935 --> 00:47:25,023
In one large trial of this technology, women with term fetuses that developed abnormal FHR patterns were assigned to conventional FHR monitoring alone or FHR monitoring plus continuous fetal pulse oximetry .

386
00:47:25,023 --> 00:47:35,173
The use of fetal pulse oximetry significantly reduced the cesarean delivery rate for nonreassuring fetal status from 10.2 to 4.5 percent.

387
00:47:35,173 --> 00:47:40,985
No neonatal benefits or adverse effects were associated with fetal pulse oximetry.

388
00:47:40,985 --> 00:47:51,373
Based on these observations, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved marketing of the Nellcor N-400 Fetal Oxygen Monitoring System.

389
00:47:51,373 --> 00:47:57,198
However, three subsequent trials compared fetal pulse oximetry with standard care.

390
00:47:57,198 --> 00:48:01,848
In each, neonatal outcomes were similar between study arms.

391
00:48:01,848 --> 00:48:08,910
In one, the addition of oximetry significantly reduced cesarean delivery rates for fetal indications .

392
00:48:08,910 --> 00:48:19,123
However, two studies found no difference in cesarean delivery rates between the two study arms (Bloom, 2006; Klauser, 2005).

393
00:48:19,123 --> 00:48:26,485
Because of these findings, in 2005, the manufacturer discontinued device sales in the United States.

394
00:48:26,485 --> 00:48:29,648
# Fetal Electrocardiography.

395
00:48:29,648 --> 00:48:34,260
As fetal hypoxia worsens, the fetal ECG changes.

396
00:48:34,260 --> 00:48:44,910
Namely, the mature fetus with hypoxemia develops an elevated ST segment and a progressive rise in the T-wave height that can be expressed as a T:QRS ratio .

397
00:48:44,910 --> 00:48:54,135
Increasing T:QRS ratios are thought to reflect fetal cardiac ability to adapt to hypoxia and appear before neurological damage.

398
00:48:54,135 --> 00:49:01,610
Further worsening of hypoxia then leads to progressively negative ST-segment deflection that creates a biphasic form.

399
00:49:01,610 --> 00:49:03,723
It is reasonable to

400
00:49:03,892 --> 00:49:09,854
consider that STsegment abnormalities might occur late in the course of fetal compromise.

401
00:49:09,804 --> 00:49:16,754
Indeed, it has been hypothesized that ST-segment changes reflect myocardial tissue hypoxia.

402
00:49:16,754 --> 00:49:19,617
Figure 24-14.

403
00:49:19,617 --> 00:49:24,542
A. Generation of T:QRS ratio from a normal ST segment.

404
00:49:24,542 --> 00:49:28,579
B. ST-segment elevation in hypoxic conditions.

405
00:49:28,579 --> 00:49:33,704
C. Biphasic ST-segment waveform with progressive fetal hypoxia.

406
00:49:33,704 --> 00:49:40,804
Because of these findings, these parameters have been evaluated as an adjunct to conventional fetal monitoring.

407
00:49:40,804 --> 00:49:47,767
The technique requires internal FHR monitoring and special equipment to process the fetal ECG.

408
00:49:47,767 --> 00:49:57,279
In 2005, the manufacturer—Neoventa Medical—received FDA approval for their STsegment analysis program named the STAN system.

409
00:49:57,279 --> 00:50:02,504
Several studies have evaluated ST-segment changes with fetal monitoring.

410
00:50:02,504 --> 00:50:13,367
In one randomized trial of 2400 pregnancies, neonatal outcomes were not improved compared with those in which conventional FHR monitoring alone was used .

411
00:50:13,367 --> 00:50:20,229
However, the cesarean delivery rate for fetal distress declined in those with ST-segment analysis.

412
00:50:20,229 --> 00:50:35,104
Subsequent studies revealed conflicting data regarding lower rates of operative delivery, fetal acidemia, and neonatal encephalopathy (AmerWåhlin, 2001; Becker, 2012; Doria, 2007).

413
00:50:35,104 --> 00:50:46,242
In a trial by the NICHD, 5532 women were randomly assigned to an STsegment analysis and 5576 to standard intrapartum management.

414
00:50:46,242 --> 00:50:52,692
The primary outcome was a composite of one or more of seven events associated with fetal compromise .

415
00:50:52,692 --> 00:50:59,142
In the ST-segment analysis group, clinician actions were directed by predetermined guidelines.

416
00:50:59,142 --> 00:51:25,592
These stipulated that intervention should be withheld, that is, expectant management adopted, for at least 60 minutes despite the presence of minimal variability; variable decelerations lasting ≥60 seconds or dropping to a level ≥60 bpm; recurrent late decelerations; or prolonged decelerations lasting >2 minutes, so long as no ST-segment abnormality was present.

417
00:51:25,592 --> 00:51:29,354
These guidelines did not pertain to the standard group.

418
00:51:29,354 --> 00:51:43,517
Notably, in the ST-segment analysis group, 55 (20 percent) of the 287 cesarean deliveries performed for fetal indications were done even though trial guidelines indicated that labor could continue.

419
00:51:43,517 --> 00:51:52,554
In these cases, physicians likely perceived the FHR patterns to reflect those formerly accepted in their usual practice as nonreassuring.

420
00:51:52,554 --> 00:51:59,292
These results showed that STAN system had no effect on neonatal outcome or cesarean delivery rates .

421
00:51:59,292 --> 00:52:03,604
A subsequent systematic review reached similar conclusions .

422
00:52:03,604 --> 00:52:11,529
These results have essentially eliminated use of ST-segment analysis in the United States, but it is still used in Europe.

423
00:52:11,529 --> 00:52:14,279
Nonreassuring Fetal Status.

424
00:52:14,279 --> 00:52:20,854
The term fetal distress is too broad and vague to be applied with any precision to clinical situations .

425
00:52:20,854 --> 00:52:30,154
Uncertainty regarding the diagnosis based on FHR pattern interpretation has given rise to descriptions such as reassuring or nonreassuring.

426
00:52:30,154 --> 00:52:34,054
The former suggests confidence in the health of the fetus.

427
00:52:34,054 --> 00:52:39,942
In contrast, a nonreassuring designation suggests inability to remove doubt.

428
00:52:39,942 --> 00:52:47,029
However, these assessments are subjective judgments that are inevitably imperfect and must be recognized as such.

429
00:52:47,029 --> 00:52:56,517
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019d) recommends use of these terms followed by a description of the findings.

430
00:52:56,517 --> 00:53:06,779
The difficulty in assigning a nonreassuring label to FHR patterns stems in part from the fact that these patterns reflect fetal physiology more so than pathology.

431
00:53:06,779 --> 00:53:12,942
Heart rate control stems from interconnected mechanisms that depend on blood flow and oxygenation.

432
00:53:12,942 --> 00:53:19,079
Moreover, these mechanisms are influenced by the preexisting state of fetal oxygenation.

433
00:53:19,079 --> 00:53:22,767
Chronic placental insufficiency is one example.

434
00:53:22,767 --> 00:53:30,817
Thus, normal labor is a process of repeated fetal hypoxic events and can infrequently lead to significant acidemia .

435
00:53:30,817 --> 00:53:33,217
# Diagnosis.

436
00:53:33,217 --> 00:53:39,517
Identification of nonreassuring fetal status based on FHR patterns is imprecise.

437
00:53:39,517 --> 00:53:46,104
One study of interobserver agreement with FHR pattern interpretation found that consensus varied .

438
00:53:46,104 --> 00:53:56,992
Specifically, experts agreed on 62 percent of normal patterns, 42 percent of suspicious patterns, and only 25 percent of pathological patterns.

439
00:53:57,148 --> 00:54:04,335
In another study, 17 experts reviewed 50 tracings on two occasions, at least 1 month apart.

440
00:54:04,285 --> 00:54:13,760
Approximately 20 percent changed their own interpretations, and approximately 25 percent did not agree with the interpretations of their colleagues .

441
00:54:13,760 --> 00:54:21,398
In an attempt to resolve this imprecision, the NICHD recommended a three-tier system for FHR pattern classification .

442
00:54:21,398 --> 00:54:28,598
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) has recommended its use.

443
00:54:28,598 --> 00:54:42,548
To assess this tiered system, Jackson and associates (2011) studied 48,444 women in labor and found that category I patterns were observed in 99.5 percent of tracings.

444
00:54:42,548 --> 00:54:52,060
Category II patterns were found in 84.1 percent, and category III patterns were seen in 0.1 percent (54 women).

445
00:54:52,060 --> 00:54:56,760
During labor, 84 percent of women had a mix of categories.

446
00:54:56,760 --> 00:55:15,810
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the American Academy of Pediatrics (2019d) has concluded that a category I or II tracing with a 5-minute Apgar score >7 or with normal arterial blood acid–base values is not consistent with an acute hypoxic-ischemic event.

447
00:55:15,810 --> 00:55:18,348
Table 24-2.

448
00:55:18,348 --> 00:55:24,798
Three-Tier Fetal Heart Rate Interpretation System Other studies have evaluated this three-tiered system.

449
00:55:24,798 --> 00:55:38,073
In one, the incidence of umbilical cord acidemia (pH ≤7.10) was correlated retrospectively with FHR characteristics during the 30 minutes preceding delivery.

450
00:55:38,073 --> 00:55:44,310
None of the three categories demonstrated a significant association with cord blood acidemia .

451
00:55:44,310 --> 00:55:54,660
Sholapurkar (2012) has challenged the validity of the three-tier system because most abnormal FHR patterns fall into the indeterminate category II.

452
00:55:54,660 --> 00:56:03,835
It was suggested that this resulted from most FHR decelerations being inappropriately classified as variable and attributed to cord compression.

453
00:56:03,835 --> 00:56:16,110
Parer and King (2010) compared this situation in the United States with that of other countries in which a consensus on classification and management has been reached by their professional societies.

454
00:56:16,110 --> 00:56:27,098
These authors note that the NICHD three-tier system is inadequate because category II contains a vast heterogenous mixture of patterns that prevents development of a management strategy.

455
00:56:27,098 --> 00:56:41,098
Weissbach and associates (2018) found that duration of category II tracings does not predict perinatal asphyxia, but the individual components of diminished variability and fetal tachycardia are predictive.

456
00:56:41,098 --> 00:56:50,473
Parer and Ikeda (2007) had previously proposed a color-coded five-tier system for both FHR interpretation and management.

457
00:56:50,473 --> 00:56:55,098
Two subsequent reports have compared the five- and three-tier systems.

458
00:56:55,098 --> 00:57:03,035
In one, the two systems were similar in FHR interpretations for tracings that were either very normal or very abnormal .

459
00:57:03,035 --> 00:57:08,798
The other study found that the five-tier system had better sensitivity than the three-tier one .

460
00:57:08,798 --> 00:57:18,523
It is apparent that despite decades of electronic FHR monitoring, a consensus on FHR pattern interpretation and diagnosis is still lacking .

461
00:57:18,523 --> 00:57:21,935
# Meconium in the Amnionic Fluid.

462
00:57:21,935 --> 00:57:29,560
Obstetricians have long realized that meconium during labor is an inaccurate predictor of fetal distress or asphyxia.

463
00:57:29,560 --> 00:57:37,898
Indeed, although 12 to 22 percent of labors are complicated by meconium, only a few are linked to neonatal mortality.

464
00:57:37,898 --> 00:57:50,510
In an investigation from Parkland Hospital, meconium was found to be a “low-risk” obstetrical hazard because the perinatal mortality rate attributable to meconium was only 1 death per 1000 live births .

465
00:57:50,510 --> 00:57:58,210
Three etiological theories may explain the tenuous association between meconium passage and neonatal mortality.

466
00:57:58,210 --> 00:58:06,135
First, fetuses may pass meconium in response to hypoxia, and meconium therefore signals fetal compromise .

467
00:58:06,135 --> 00:58:14,773
Second, however, in utero passage of meconium may represent normal gastrointestinal tract maturation under neural control .

468
00:58:14,773 --> 00:58:24,898
A final theory posits that common but transient umbilical cord entrapment leads to vagal stimulation, increased bowel peristalsis, and meconium passage .

469
00:58:24,898 --> 00:58:35,698
Jovanovic and Nguyen (1989) observed that meconium aspirated into fetal lungs during gasping caused aspiration syndrome only in asphyxiated animals.

470
00:58:35,698 --> 00:58:42,648
Ramin and associates (1996) studied almost 8000 pregnancies with meconium-stained

471
00:58:42,796 --> 00:58:46,433
amnionic fluid delivered at Parkland Hospital.

472
00:58:46,383 --> 00:58:52,571
Meconium aspiration syndrome was significantly associated with fetal acidemia at birth.

473
00:58:52,571 --> 00:59:01,408
Further analysis of umbilical blood gases suggested that the fetal compromise associated with meconium aspiration syndrome was an acute event.

474
00:59:01,408 --> 00:59:09,683
This is because most acidemic fetuses had abnormally elevated PCO2 values rather than a pure metabolic acidemia.

475
00:59:09,683 --> 00:59:23,858
Other significant correlates of aspiration included cesarean delivery, forceps to expedite delivery, intrapartum FHR abnormalities, depressed Apgar scores, and need for assisted ventilation at delivery.

476
00:59:23,858 --> 00:59:40,071
Ramin and colleagues (1996) hypothesized that the pathophysiology of meconium aspiration syndrome includes, but is not limited to, fetal hypercarbia, which stimulates fetal respiration leading to aspiration of meconium into the alveoli.

477
00:59:40,071 --> 00:59:45,808
Lung parenchymal injury is secondary to acidemia-induced alveolar cell damage.

478
00:59:45,808 --> 00:59:55,408
In this pathophysiological scenario, meconium in amnionic fluid is a fetal environmental hazard rather than a marker of preexisting compromise.

479
00:59:55,408 --> 01:00:07,933
This proposed pathophysiological sequence is not all-inclusive, because it does not account for approximately half of the cases of meconium aspiration syndrome in which the fetus is not acidemic at birth.

480
01:00:07,933 --> 01:00:18,483
Thus, the high incidence of meconium observed in the amnionic fluid during labor often represents normal physiological fetal passage of gastrointestinal contents.

481
01:00:18,483 --> 01:00:26,183
Although normal, such meconium becomes an environmental hazard when fetal acidemia from hypercarbia supervenes.

482
01:00:26,183 --> 01:00:34,633
Importantly, such acidemia occurs acutely, and therefore meconium aspiration is unpredictable and likely unpreventable.

483
01:00:34,633 --> 01:00:42,483
Growing evidence indicates that many newborns with meconium aspiration syndrome have suffered chronic hypoxia before birth .

484
01:00:42,483 --> 01:00:57,858
In one study, 60 percent of neonates diagnosed with meconium aspiration syndrome had umbilical artery blood pH ≥7.20, which implies that the syndrome was unrelated to the neonatal condition at delivery .

485
01:00:57,858 --> 01:01:17,471
Similarly, comorbid markers of chronic hypoxia, such as elevated fetal erythropoietin levels and increased nucleated red blood cell counts in newborns, suggest that chronic hypoxia is involved in many meconium aspiration syndrome cases (Dollberg, 2001; Jazayeri, 2000).

486
01:01:17,471 --> 01:01:27,783
Previously, routine obstetrical management of a newborn with meconiumstained amnionic fluid included intrapartum suctioning of the oropharynx and nasopharynx.

487
01:01:27,783 --> 01:01:44,746
Current recommendations state that newborns with meconiumstained amnionic fluid, regardless of their vigor, should no longer routinely receive intrapartum suctioning (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021; Wyckoff, 2015).

488
01:01:44,746 --> 01:01:48,583
Suctioning is reserved for those with airway obstruction.

489
01:01:48,583 --> 01:01:54,646
They also recommend that an appropriately credentialed team with full resuscitation skills be available .

490
01:01:54,646 --> 01:01:57,308
# Management Options.

491
01:01:57,308 --> 01:02:03,971
Initial management of variant FHR patterns aims to correct any fetal insult, if possible.

492
01:02:03,971 --> 01:02:07,921
Suggestions are listed in Table 24-3.

493
01:02:07,921 --> 01:02:13,946
The woman is moved to a lateral position, and supplemental oxygen is provided by mask.

494
01:02:13,946 --> 01:02:23,008
Correcting maternal hypotension caused by regional analgesia and discontinuing oxytocin both serve to improve uteroplacental perfusion.

495
01:02:23,008 --> 01:02:28,071
Vaginal examination excludes a prolapsed cord or impending delivery.

496
01:02:28,071 --> 01:02:38,021
Simpson and James (2005) assessed the benefits of three maneuvers in 52 women with fetal oxygen saturation sensors already in place.

497
01:02:38,021 --> 01:02:53,721
They provided IV hydration with 500 to 1000 mL of lactated Ringer solution given over 20 minutes; lateral versus supine positioning; and administration of supplemental oxygen at 10 L/min using a nonrebreathing mask.

498
01:02:53,721 --> 01:02:58,958
Each of these maneuvers significantly raised fetal oxygen saturation levels.

499
01:02:58,958 --> 01:03:15,208
A randomized trial comparing oxygen via face mask to room air showed no difference in resolution of recurrent decelerations (75 percent versus 86 percent), time to resolution of recurrent decelerations, and total deceleration area .

500
01:03:15,208 --> 01:03:17,771
Table 24-3.

501
01:03:17,771 --> 01:03:28,221
Some Resuscitative Measures for Category II or Category III Tracings Tocolysis Terbutaline sulfate given to relax the uterus can be a temporizing maneuver in the

502
01:03:28,372 --> 01:03:32,772
management of nonreassuring FHR patterns during labor.

503
01:03:32,722 --> 01:03:42,372
A single 250-μg IV or subcutaneous injection is used to inhibit uterine contractions and thereby improve fetal oxygenation.

504
01:03:42,372 --> 01:03:54,622
One study described 368 pregnancies in which terbutaline was used to relieve contractions in preparation for cesarean delivery that was already planned for nonreassuring fetal status .

505
01:03:54,622 --> 01:04:01,484
Compared with a group not receiving tocolysis, the treated group had fewer 5-minute Apgar scores <7.

506
01:04:01,484 --> 01:04:08,847
Small IV doses of nitroglycerin —60 to 180 μg—also have reported benefit .

507
01:04:08,847 --> 01:04:16,847
In one comparator study, both agents resolved tachysystole, but nitroglycerin lowered mean arterial blood pressures .

508
01:04:16,847 --> 01:04:27,559
Two systematic reviews both noted that data were limited and reached differing views on tocolysis benefits (Bullens, 2015; Leathersich, 2018).

509
01:04:27,559 --> 01:04:39,047
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) cites that evidence is insufficient to recommend tocolysis for nonreassuring FHR patterns.

510
01:04:39,047 --> 01:04:46,259
We consider terbutaline during labor stimulation to resolve tachysystole-associated prolonged decelerations.

511
01:04:46,259 --> 01:04:55,184
However, the chance of success is balanced against terbutaline’s side effects should cesarean delivery be needed for unresolved bradycardia.

512
01:04:55,184 --> 01:05:03,222
Namely, β agonist–related maternal tachycardia exacerbates the tachycardia associated with surgery itself.

513
01:05:03,222 --> 01:05:14,059
We consider maternal cardiopulmonary disease, poorly controlled hyperthyroidism, and maternal or fetal tachycardia with loss of variability to be contraindications.

514
01:05:14,059 --> 01:05:28,684
Amnioinfusion Early studies infused saline through an intrauterine pressure catheter in laboring women who had either variable or prolonged decelerations attributed to cord compression (Miyazaki, 1983, 1985).

515
01:05:28,684 --> 01:05:35,547
Such therapy improved the FHR pattern and decreased cesarean delivery rates for fetal indications.

516
01:05:35,547 --> 01:05:51,534
For variable decelerations, other studies also support amnioinfusion to reduce variable deceleration frequency, improve neonatal outcome, and lower cesarean delivery rates (Hofmeyr, 2012; Raghuraman, 2020a).

517
01:05:51,534 --> 01:06:06,797
The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) conclude that amnioinfusion is a reasonable approach to treat repetitive variable decelerations but not late decelerations.

518
01:06:06,797 --> 01:06:20,822
As a prophylactic tool in known cases of oligohydramnios, evidence does not support amnioinfusion to prevent cord compressionrelated decelerations (Nageotte, 1991; Ogundipe, 1994).

519
01:06:20,822 --> 01:06:29,684
Last, attempts to flush out or dilute thick meconium are not recommended (Fraser, 2005; Xu, 2007).

520
01:06:29,684 --> 01:06:46,059
Despite differing amnioinfusion protocols, most provide a 500- to 800mL bolus of warmed normal saline, which is followed by a continuous infusion of 3 mL/min (Owen, 1990; Pressman, 1996).

521
01:06:46,059 --> 01:06:58,234
Rinehart and colleagues (2000) administered a 500-mL bolus of normal saline at room temperature alone or this bolus plus a continuous infusion at 3 mL/min.

522
01:06:58,234 --> 01:07:04,722
They included 65 women with variable decelerations and found neither method to be superior.

523
01:07:04,722 --> 01:07:13,697
Potential amnioinfusion complications are summarized in Table 24-4 from a U.S. survey of fellowship and residency directors .

524
01:07:13,697 --> 01:07:16,284
Table 24-4.

525
01:07:16,284 --> 01:07:25,959
Complications Associated with Amnioinfusion from a Survey of 186 Obstetrical Units # Fetal Heart Rate Patterns and Brain Injury.

526
01:07:25,959 --> 01:07:34,809
Studies attempting to correlate FHR patterns with brain injury have primarily examined infants identified in medicolegal actions.

527
01:07:34,809 --> 01:07:48,372
Phelan and Ahn (1994) reported that among 48 fetuses later found to be neurologically impaired, a persistent nonreactive FHR tracing was already present at the time of admission in 70 percent.

528
01:07:48,372 --> 01:07:54,547
They concluded that fetal neurological injury occurred predominantly before arrival to the hospital.

529
01:07:54,547 --> 01:08:05,809
When they looked retrospectively at FHR patterns in 209 brain-injured newborns, they concluded that there was not a single unique pattern associated with fetal neurological injury .

530
01:08:05,809 --> 01:08:11,834
Others have reported the same conclusion (Nakao, 2020; Phelan, 2000).

531
01:08:11,834 --> 01:08:23,134
In a review of literature published between 1966 and 2006 on the effect of FHR monitoring to prevent perinatal brain injury, the summary found no benefit .

532
01:08:23,134 --> 01:08:27,022
FHR patterns necessary for perinatal brain

533
01:08:27,172 --> 01:08:30,034
damage have been studied in animals.

534
01:08:29,984 --> 01:08:37,059
Myers (1972) described the effects of complete and partial asphyxia in rhesus monkeys .

535
01:08:37,059 --> 01:08:43,947
Complete asphyxia was produced by total occlusion of umbilical blood flow that led to prolonged deceleration.

536
01:08:43,947 --> 01:08:53,347
Fetal arterial pH did not drop to 7.0 until approximately 8 minutes after complete cessation of oxygenation and umbilical flow.

537
01:08:53,347 --> 01:09:00,684
At least 10 minutes of such prolonged deceleration were required before surviving fetuses demonstrated brain damage.

538
01:09:00,684 --> 01:09:03,522
Figure 24-15.

539
01:09:03,522 --> 01:09:12,272
Prolonged deceleration in a rhesus monkey shown with blood pressure and biochemical changes during total occlusion of umbilical cord blood flow.

540
01:09:12,272 --> 01:09:23,034
The most common FHR pattern during labor—due to umbilical cord occlusion—requires considerable time to significantly affect the fetus in experimental animals.

541
01:09:23,034 --> 01:09:31,172
Clapp and colleagues (1988) partially occluded the umbilical cord for 1 minute every 3 minutes in fetal sheep.

542
01:09:31,172 --> 01:09:43,784
Rocha and associates (2004) totally occluded the umbilical cord for 90 seconds every 30 minutes for 3 to 5 hours a day for 4 days without producing necrotic brain cell injury.

543
01:09:43,784 --> 01:09:52,909
Results from such studies suggest that the effects of umbilical cord entrapment depend on the degree, the duration, and the frequency of such occlusions.

544
01:09:52,909 --> 01:10:03,522
The contribution of intrapartum events to subsequent neurological handicaps has been greatly overestimated, as discussed in Chapter 33 (p. 601).

545
01:10:03,522 --> 01:10:09,497
For brain damage, the fetus must be exposed to much more than a brief period of hypoxia.

546
01:10:09,497 --> 01:10:16,134
Moreover, the hypoxia must cause profound, just barely sublethal metabolic acidemia.

547
01:10:16,134 --> 01:10:38,072
Because of this, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019d) has recommended umbilical cord blood gases be obtained when cesarean delivery is performed for fetal compromise or when a low 5-minute Apgar score, severe fetal-growth restriction, an abnormal FHR tracing, or multifetal gestation is present .

548
01:10:38,072 --> 01:10:46,547
Until recently, the prognosis for moderately affected newborns with hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (HIE) was poor.

549
01:10:46,547 --> 01:10:56,509
More recently, studies show that brain cooling administered to newborns suffering neonatal HIE could ameliorate the development of subsequent cerebral palsy .

550
01:10:56,509 --> 01:11:00,822
# Benefits of Electronic Fetal Heart Rate Monitoring.

551
01:11:00,822 --> 01:11:08,497
Several false assumptions underlie the expectation of improved perinatal outcome with electronic FHR monitoring.

552
01:11:08,497 --> 01:11:17,947
One is that nonreassuring fetal status is a slowly developing phenomenon and that electronic monitoring permits early detection of the compromised fetus.

553
01:11:17,947 --> 01:11:22,397
Another presumption is that all fetal injury develops in the hospital.

554
01:11:22,397 --> 01:11:29,172
In reality, most neurologically damaged fetuses suffer insults before arrival at labor units.

555
01:11:29,172 --> 01:11:35,372
The very term fetal monitor implies that this inanimate technology in some fashion “monitors.

556
01:11:35,372 --> 01:11:44,934
The assumption is made that if a dead or damaged fetus is delivered, the tracing strip must provide some clue, because this device was monitoring fetal condition.

557
01:11:44,934 --> 01:11:50,972
These assumptions led to expectations that all neonatal deaths or injuries were preventable.

558
01:11:50,972 --> 01:11:59,572
By the end of the 1970s, questions regarding the efficacy, safety, and costs of electronic monitoring were being voiced.

559
01:11:59,572 --> 01:12:07,134
Banta and Thacker (2002) reviewed the benefits, or lack thereof, of electronic FHR monitoring.

560
01:12:07,134 --> 01:12:22,534
More recently, a review of 13 randomized trials involving more than 37,000 women concluded that electronic FHR monitoring was associated with fewer neonatal seizures but a higher rate of cesarean and operative vaginal deliveries .

561
01:12:22,534 --> 01:12:28,084
However, rates of perinatal mortality or cerebral palsy did not decline.

562
01:12:28,084 --> 01:12:35,522
Grimes and Peipert (2010) concluded that such monitoring has failed as a public health screening program.

563
01:12:35,522 --> 01:12:47,584
They noted that the positive-predictive value of electronic FHR monitoring for fetal death in labor or cerebral palsy is near zero and reflects that “almost every positive test result is wrong.

564
01:12:47,584 --> 01:12:57,147
Epidemiologically, two large studies in the United States reported a decline in neonatal mortality rates due to electronic FHR monitoring.

565
01:12:57,147 --> 01:13:05,134
However, the highest effect was seen in preterm neonates (Ananth, 2013; Chen, 2011).

566
01:13:05,134 --> 01:13:08,209
Of note, caution must be used in

567
01:13:08,380 --> 01:13:14,742
interpreting these studies, as epidemiological association does not establish causation .

568
01:13:14,692 --> 01:13:20,805
At admission for labor, most women with low-risk pregnancies are monitored for a short time.

569
01:13:20,805 --> 01:13:33,767
In one study, 3752 low-risk women in spontaneous labor were randomly assigned at admission to either FHR auscultation or 20 minutes of electronic FHR monitoring .

570
01:13:33,767 --> 01:13:38,642
Electronic FHR monitoring did not improve neonatal outcome.

571
01:13:38,642 --> 01:13:44,967
Moreover, its use resulted in a greater number of interventions, including operative delivery.

572
01:13:44,967 --> 01:13:48,705
A similar study echoed these neonatal outcomes .

573
01:13:48,705 --> 01:13:59,367
Last, admission FHR monitoring for low-risk pregnancy is associated with a higher cesarean delivery rate but not improved neonatal outcomes in one Cochrane review .

574
01:13:59,367 --> 01:14:08,992
At Parkland Hospital in July 1982, an investigation began to ascertain whether all women during labor should undergo electronic monitoring .

575
01:14:08,992 --> 01:14:18,405
In alternating months, universal electronic FHR monitoring was rotated with selective FHR monitoring, which was the prevailing practice.

576
01:14:18,405 --> 01:14:32,717
During the 3-year investigation, more than 17,000 labors were managed using universal electronic FHR monitoring, and these outcomes were compared with a similar-sized cohort of women selectively monitored electronically.

577
01:14:32,717 --> 01:14:37,217
No significant differences were found in any perinatal outcome.

578
01:14:37,217 --> 01:14:44,780
With universal monitoring, a small but significant increase in the cesarean delivery rate for fetal distress was noted.

579
01:14:44,780 --> 01:14:56,767
Thus, greater application of electronic FHR monitoring at Parkland Hospital did not improve perinatal results but did slightly raise the frequency of cesarean delivery for fetal distress.

580
01:14:56,767 --> 01:15:07,030
More recently, a Cochrane Database review found that intermittent auscultation was associated with a higher cesarean delivery rate compared with continuous monitoring .

581
01:15:07,030 --> 01:15:09,905
# Current Recommendations.

582
01:15:09,905 --> 01:15:24,367
Methods most commonly used for intrapartum FHR monitoring include intermittent auscultation with a fetal stethoscope or a Doppler ultrasound device, or continuous electronic monitoring of FHR and uterine contractions.

583
01:15:24,367 --> 01:15:32,667
Evidence has not identified the most effective method nor the frequency or duration of fetal surveillance that ensures optimum results.

584
01:15:32,667 --> 01:15:44,867
That said, Table 24-5 summarizes current guidelines from the American Academy of Pediatrics and American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017).

585
01:15:44,867 --> 01:15:54,942
Intermittent auscultation or continuous electronic monitoring is considered an acceptable method of intrapartum surveillance in both low- and high-risk pregnancies.

586
01:15:54,942 --> 01:16:01,967
The recommended interval between checking the heart rate, however, is longer in the uncomplicated pregnancy.

587
01:16:01,967 --> 01:16:08,992
When auscultation is used, it is recommended that it be performed after a contraction and for 60 seconds.

588
01:16:08,992 --> 01:16:15,392
It also is recommended that a 1-to-1 nurse–patient ratio be used if auscultation is employed.

589
01:16:15,392 --> 01:16:29,080
The position taken by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) acknowledges that available data do not show a clear benefit to the use of electronic monitoring over intermittent auscultation.

590
01:16:29,080 --> 01:16:34,992
At Parkland Hospital, all high-risk labors are continuously monitored electronically.

591
01:16:34,992 --> 01:16:46,580
In low-risk pregnancies, both intermittent auscultation and continuous electronic monitoring are used depending on clinical circumstances, including the woman’s desire to ambulate.

592
01:16:46,580 --> 01:16:49,305
Table 24-5.

593
01:16:49,305 --> 01:16:55,680
Guidelines for Methods of Intrapartum Fetal Heart Rate Monitoring Intrapartum Surveillance Of Uterine.

594
01:16:55,680 --> 01:16:57,367
Activity.

595
01:16:57,367 --> 01:17:07,005
Analysis of electronically measured uterine activity permits some generalities concerning the relationship of certain contraction patterns and labor outcome.

596
01:17:07,005 --> 01:17:12,130
However, uterine muscle efficiency to bring about delivery varies greatly.

597
01:17:12,130 --> 01:17:19,380
Thus, caution should be exercised before diagnosing true labor or its absence solely from a monitor tracing.

598
01:17:19,380 --> 01:17:26,517
With internal monitoring of contractions, amnionic fluid pressure is measured between and during contractions.

599
01:17:26,517 --> 01:17:33,030
An intrauterine pressure catheter is placed into the uterus with its distal tip above the presenting part.

600
01:17:33,030 --> 01:17:40,142
The catheter tip contains a pressure sensor and allows calculation of intrauterine pressure with each contraction .

601
01:17:40,142 --> 01:17:43,042
With external monitoring, uterine

602
01:17:43,204 --> 01:17:52,304
contractions can be measured by a displacement transducer in which the transducer button, or “plunger,” is held against the maternal abdominal wall.

603
01:17:52,254 --> 01:17:57,904
As the uterus contracts, the button moves in proportion to the strength of the contraction.

604
01:17:57,904 --> 01:18:05,104
This movement is converted into a measurable electrical signal that indicates the relative intensity of the contraction.

605
01:18:05,104 --> 01:18:11,229
It has generally been accepted that internal monitoring provided a more accurate measure of intensity.

606
01:18:11,229 --> 01:18:24,579
That said, in a randomized trial comparing internal versus external monitoring of uterine contractions in 1456 women, the two methods yielded equivalent operative delivery rates and neonatal outcomes .

607
01:18:24,579 --> 01:18:27,416
Figure 24-16.

608
01:18:27,416 --> 01:18:33,166
Placement of an intrauterine pressure catheter to monitor contractions and their pressures.

609
01:18:33,166 --> 01:18:41,091
The catheter, contained within the light blue introducer, is inserted into the uterus and placed along one side of the fetal head.

610
01:18:41,091 --> 01:18:46,791
The catheter is then gently advanced into the uterus, and the introducer is withdrawn.

611
01:18:46,791 --> 01:18:49,916
# Patterns of Uterine Activity.

612
01:18:49,916 --> 01:19:01,104
Caldeyro-Barcia and Poseiro (1960), from Montevideo, Uruguay, were pioneers in elucidating the patterns of spontaneous uterine activity throughout pregnancy.

613
01:19:01,104 --> 01:19:06,804
Waves of uterine activity were usually measured using intraamnionic pressure catheters.

614
01:19:06,804 --> 01:19:15,279
But early in their studies, as many as four simultaneous intramyometrial microballoons also were used to record uterine pressure.

615
01:19:15,279 --> 01:19:21,254
Contraction intensity was defined as the rise in this pressure above a resting pressure baseline.

616
01:19:21,254 --> 01:19:27,916
These investigators also introduced the concept of Montevideo units to define uterine activity .

617
01:19:27,916 --> 01:19:37,754
With this definition, uterine performance is the product of contraction intensity in mm Hg multiplied by the number of contractions in a 10-minute span.

618
01:19:37,754 --> 01:19:47,304
For example, three contractions in 10 minutes, each with 50-mm Hg intensity, would equal 150 Montevideo units.

619
01:19:47,304 --> 01:19:59,266
According to Caldeyro-Barcia and Poseiro (1960), clinical labor usually commences when uterine activity reaches values between 80 and 120 Montevideo units.

620
01:19:59,266 --> 01:20:05,966
This translates into approximately three contractions of 40 mm Hg every 10 minutes.

621
01:20:05,966 --> 01:20:12,791
Importantly, no clear-cut division marks labor onset, which is a gradual and progressive transition.

622
01:20:12,791 --> 01:20:24,554
In first-stage labor, uterine contractions progressively grow in intensity from approximately 25 mm Hg at labor commencement to 50 mm Hg at its end.

623
01:20:24,554 --> 01:20:40,166
At the same time, the frequency advances from three to five contractions per 10 minutes, and uterine baseline tone rises from 8 to 12 mm Hg. Uterine activity is further enhanced during second-stage labor, aided by maternal pushing.

624
01:20:40,166 --> 01:20:50,004
Contraction intensity of 80 to 100 mm Hg is typical, and the uterus contracts as frequently as five to six times each 10 minutes.

625
01:20:50,004 --> 01:21:01,141
Hauth and coworkers (1986) quantified uterine contraction pressures in 109 women at term who received oxytocin for labor induction or augmentation.

626
01:21:01,141 --> 01:21:10,354
Most of these women achieved 200 to 225 Montevideo units, and 40 percent had up to 300 units to effect delivery.

627
01:21:10,354 --> 01:21:18,704
The authors suggested that these levels of uterine activity should be sought before consideration of cesarean delivery for presumed dystocia .

628
01:21:18,704 --> 01:21:32,504
Interestingly, the duration of uterine contractions—60 to 80 seconds— does not lengthen appreciably from early active labor through the second stage (Bakker, 2007; Pontonnier, 1975).

629
01:21:32,504 --> 01:21:38,191
Presumably, this duration constancy serves fetal respiratory gas exchange.

630
01:21:38,191 --> 01:21:46,966
During a uterine contraction, as the intrauterine pressure exceeds that of the intervillous space, respiratory gas exchange is halted.

631
01:21:46,966 --> 01:21:54,541
This leads to functional fetal “breath holding,” which has a 60- to 80-second limit that remains relatively constant.

632
01:21:54,541 --> 01:22:06,066
Caldeyro-Barcia and Poseiro (1960) also observed empirically that uterine contractions are clinically palpable only after their intensity exceeds 10 mm Hg.

633
01:22:06,066 --> 01:22:14,854
Moreover, until the intensity of contractions reaches 40 mm Hg, the uterine wall can readily be depressed by the finger.

634
01:22:14,854 --> 01:22:21,179
At greater intensities, the uterine wall then becomes so hard that it resists easy depression.

635
01:22:21,179 --> 01:22:26,304
Uterine contractions usually are not associated with pain until their strength

636
01:22:26,452 --> 01:22:35,352
exceeds 15 mm Hg. Presumably, this is the minimum pressure required to distend the lower uterine segment and cervix.

637
01:22:35,302 --> 01:22:41,714
Hendricks (1968) observed that “the clinician makes great demands upon the uterus.

638
01:22:41,714 --> 01:22:54,589
The uterus is expected to remain well relaxed during pregnancy, to contract effectively but intermittently during labor, and then to remain in a state of almost constant contraction for several hours postpartum.

639
01:22:54,589 --> 01:23:00,614
Figure 2417 demonstrates an example of normal uterine activity during labor.

640
01:23:00,614 --> 01:23:05,977
Uterine activity progressively and gradually rises from early through late labor.

641
01:23:05,977 --> 01:23:12,952
Interestingly, uterine contractions after birth are identical to those resulting in delivery of the newborn.

642
01:23:12,952 --> 01:23:20,114
Logically, the uterus that performs poorly before delivery is also prone to atony and puerperal hemorrhage.

643
01:23:20,114 --> 01:23:23,039
Figure 24-17.

644
01:23:23,039 --> 01:23:26,814
Intrauterine pressure recorded through a single catheter.

645
01:23:26,814 --> 01:23:28,777
A. Prelabor.

646
01:23:28,777 --> 01:23:30,877
B. Early labor.

647
01:23:30,877 --> 01:23:33,164
C. Active labor.

648
01:23:33,164 --> 01:23:35,189
D. Late labor.

649
01:23:35,189 --> 01:23:39,164
E. Spontaneous activity ½ hour postpartum.

650
01:23:39,164 --> 01:23:43,627
F. Spontaneous activity 2½ hours postpartum.

651
01:23:43,627 --> 01:23:49,714
Redrawn from Hendricks CH: Uterine contractility changes in the early puerperium.

652
01:23:49,714 --> 01:23:56,402
Clin Obstet Gynecol 1968 Mar;11(1):125–144.

653
01:23:56,402 --> 01:23:59,827
# Uterine Contraction Terminology.

654
01:23:59,827 --> 01:24:08,739
Terms to describe uterine contractions have been recommended by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b).

655
01:24:08,739 --> 01:24:16,389
Normal uterine activity is defined as five or fewer contractions in 10 minutes, averaged during a 30-minute span.

656
01:24:16,389 --> 01:24:22,214
Tachysystole is more than five contractions in 10 minutes, averaged over 30 minutes.

657
01:24:22,214 --> 01:24:26,552
Tachysystole can be applied to spontaneous or induced labor.

658
01:24:26,552 --> 01:24:29,727
The term hyperstimulation was abandoned.

659
01:24:29,727 --> 01:24:41,077
Stewart and associates (2012) prospectively studied uterine tachysystole in 584 women undergoing labor induction with misoprostol at Parkland Hospital.

660
01:24:41,077 --> 01:24:49,489
A higher rate of adverse neonatal outcomes was not associated with an increasing number of contractions per 10 minutes or per 30 minutes.

661
01:24:49,489 --> 01:24:57,602
Counts of six or more contractions in 10 minutes, however, were significantly associated with FHR decelerations.

662
01:24:57,602 --> 01:25:01,627
# Electronic Fetal Monitoring Complications.

663
01:25:01,627 --> 01:25:11,464
Electrodes for FHR evaluation and catheters for uterine contraction measurement are both associated with infrequent but potentially serious complications.

664
01:25:11,464 --> 01:25:18,177
Rarely, an intrauterine pressure catheter may lacerate a fetal vessel in the placenta during placement.

665
01:25:18,177 --> 01:25:28,602
Also with insertion, placental and possibly uterine perforation can cause hemorrhage, abruption, and spurious recordings that have resulted in inappropriate management.

666
01:25:28,602 --> 01:25:33,852
Severe cord compression has been described from entanglement with the pressure catheter.

667
01:25:33,852 --> 01:25:39,314
Injury to the fetal scalp or breech by a FHR electrode is rarely severe.

668
01:25:39,314 --> 01:25:44,227
However, face presentations pose risk for eye or mouth trauma.

669
01:25:44,227 --> 01:25:49,752
Both the fetus and the mother may be at greater risk of infection from internal monitoring .

670
01:25:49,752 --> 01:26:02,052
Scalp wounds from the electrode may become infected, but subsequent cranial osteomyelitis is rare (Brook, 2005; Eggink, 2004; McGregor, 1989).

671
01:26:02,052 --> 01:26:15,477
Certain maternal infections that include human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), herpes simplex virus, and hepatitis B and C virus are relative contraindications to internal FHR monitoring .

